sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch01-05> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Across the world of simple programs the same basic forms of behavior occur repeatedly almost independently of the underlying details of the rules, suggesting universal principles of overall behavior that should also apply to systems throughout nature.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The same basic forms of behavior recur in simple programs regardless of details";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 1, “The Foundations for a New Kind of Science”; section “An Outline of Basic Ideas” (from p. 1), p. 4. Scope: simple programs of many types (cellular automata, Turing machines, substitution systems, etc.) and, by extension, natural systems"
}
